  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several membrane-based battery concepts and how their active materials are arranged and moved during operation. Across the top, “rocking chair” and “dual ion” systems show two solid grid-like electrodes separated by a membrane region; the arrows indicate reversible charging and discharging. In the rocking-chair case, ions shuttle back and forth between two redox-active solids. In the dual-ion case, dissolved positive and negative ions in the electrolyte appear to migrate toward opposite electrodes during cycling.\n\nThe middle row, labeled “multielectrolyte,” adds multiple liquid compartments and selective membranes. Different colored electrolytes and ions are kept apart, while active species seem to deposit into or occupy porous electrode structures at the sides. This layout emphasizes spatial separation of chemistries using membranes with different selectivities.\n\nThe bottom row, “multiphase,” extends this idea to tanks and flow-assisted operation. Stirred reservoirs contain suspended or dissolved redox-active materials, which are circulated through membrane-separated electrochemical cells. One example shows particles crossing or interacting near a separator; another shows ions in one tank and solid electrode particles in another. Overall, the figure highlights a progression from conventional intercalation electrodes to more complex membrane, electrolyte, and phase-separated architectures for charge storage.",
